            "<strong>Infertility</strong> is the failure to achieve a clinical pregnancy after <strong>12 months or more</strong> of regular unprotected intercourse; it is <strong>primary</strong> when the woman has never conceived and <strong>secondary</strong> when she has conceived before, whatever the outcome of that pregnancy. Evaluation begins earlier — after six months — when the woman is 35 or older, and at once when there is a known cause such as amenorrhoea, previous pelvic infection or a known male factor. About one couple in six or seven worldwide experiences infertility, and in sub-Saharan Africa the pattern is distinctive: <strong>secondary infertility predominates</strong>, and a large share of both female and male infertility follows <strong>infection</strong> — untreated gonorrhoea and chlamydia, pelvic inflammatory disease, septic abortion, puerperal sepsis, tuberculosis of the genital tract and schistosomiasis in the woman; gonorrhoea, chlamydia, mumps orchitis and epididymitis in the man. This ‘infertility belt’ pattern is why infection prevention and treatment (module 5) is also fertility care. The second truth to carry into every consultation is that the man contributes to the cause in <strong>about half of couples</strong> — a male factor alone in around 20 to 30 per cent and combined with a female factor in another 20 to 30 per cent — so a woman who arrives alone to be ‘checked’ is sent home with an appointment for her husband, not with a prescription. The third is that infertility in Ghana is a social catastrophe before it is a medical diagnosis: the woman is blamed by her in-laws, threatened with a co-wife or divorce, excluded from women's ceremonies and sometimes from inheritance, called a witch, and may spend years and her savings on prayer camps, herbalists and injections before she reaches a clinic — by which time she is often over 35 and the tubes are long since scarred.",
