            "For incomplete and missed miscarriage the woman is offered the three options and chooses, with the nurse's guidance on safety. <strong>Expectant management</strong> suits a stable woman with light bleeding and a small amount of retained tissue who can return easily; most complete within two weeks, with a pregnancy test at three weeks to confirm. <strong>Medical management with misoprostol</strong> — 600 micrograms orally or 400 micrograms sublingually as a single dose for incomplete miscarriage; 800 micrograms vaginally or sublingually, repeated if needed, for missed miscarriage, ideally after mifepristone — is effective in 80 to 95 per cent, can be given at the health centre, and the woman is told to expect cramping and bleeding heavier than a period for a few hours, fever and chills within the first day as a drug effect, and to return for bleeding that soaks two pads an hour for two hours, fever after 24 hours, or offensive discharge. <strong>Manual vacuum aspiration (MVA)</strong> with the Ipas double-valve syringe and flexible cannulae is the surgical method of choice up to 12 to 14 weeks and is within the scope of trained midwives in Ghana: a paracervical block with lidocaine and oral analgesia, no-touch technique, the cannula matched to uterine size, the products inspected for completeness (villi and sac confirm an intrauterine pregnancy; their absence raises the question of ectopic), and the woman observed for an hour. It is indicated whenever bleeding is heavy, infection is present, medical treatment has failed or the woman prefers it, and it has replaced sharp curettage, which causes more perforation, infection and Asherman syndrome. Every woman receives analgesia, <strong>anti-D immunoglobulin</strong> if she is rhesus negative and has a miscarriage managed surgically or medically or is beyond 12 weeks (250 IU before 20 weeks; many Ghanaian facilities use the 300 microgram/1,500 IU preparation), iron if anaemic, and before she leaves: an explanation that a miscarriage is nobody's fault and usually reflects a chromosomal abnormality the body recognised, acknowledgement of her loss, the warning signs, a review date, and <strong>family planning</strong>, since ovulation can return within two weeks and every method including the intrauterine device can be started on the day.",
