          alt: "Hypovolaemic shock — why blood pressure falls last. The young patient compensates beautifully, then collapses without warning. Blood lost up to 15 per cent: pulse under 100, blood pressure normal, slightly anxious, urine normal. 15 to 30 per cent: pulse over 100, blood pressure still normal but pulse pressure narrows, anxious, restless, urine reduced. 30 to 40 per cent: pulse over 120, weak, blood pressure falling at last, confused, scanty urine. Over 40 per cent: pulse over 140, thready, very low blood pressure, lethargic, unconscious, no urine. Read the pulse and the mind, not the blood pressure. Restlessness is hypoxia, not anxiety — never sedate it. Immediate action: lay flat, raise the legs, oxygen, two large cannulae, warmed fluid, stop the bleeding with direct pressure, keep the patient warm. Concealed bleeding: chest, abdomen, pelvis, both thighs and the floor — litres can be lost with nothing visible. No wound does not mean no haemorrhage.",
